A||N||9||jaguiler||Cruise control, turn signal stalk....||||5||
Z||000000||jaguiler||04-17-2001||03:31 PM||||Hi everyone,<P>I was fooling around with my cruise control.  I got the car cruising once or twice... everyhting seemed like it was working.  But I can not get it to cruise again.  I am willing to bet it is the stalk itself on the steering col.  My question is does this stalk clearly twist off and unplug when I buy a replacement GM part? or do I have to take apart the whole steering col.?<P>Thanks.<BR>See you all in Carlise!<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||209.246.221.97||reg||5
Z||000001||fierojunky||04-17-2001||03:53 PM||||Everywhere i read says your supposed to take the column apart - i managed to replace mine without taking the column apart or even the stering wheel off.  I just took the stalk off - cut the cable from the old stalk - duct taped the new cable to the old one and pulled like hell (pulling the old cable out the bottom of the column) worked for me -  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> dont know if anyone else used this method.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||04-17-2001||05:03 PM||||Fierojunky is right don't take your column apart and use the old wire to pull the new wire. I wish I had used duct tape when I did it but after the tape let go, I was able to thread a thin steel wire down and use it to pull the new wire.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||04-17-2001||05:28 PM||||Are you sure it's not one of the switches down on the peddles not allowing the cruise to operate?  <BR>Those are easy to replace if one or more of those were failing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake2m4||04-17-2001||05:40 PM||||The turn signal stalk comes out pretty easily.  If you look and fiddle a little it shouldn't be a problem.  I got it out after fooling with it for about a minute.  then ya cut and solder the wires.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bluebottlejunky||04-17-2001||05:55 PM||||so that is what that thingy is..wow..brain fart.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||04-17-2001||10:30 PM||||You can get a replacement cruise stalk from Rodney Dickman at a very reasonable cost.  When you get the new one, just unplug the connector under the dash and plug in the new one.  That will tell you if the original is bad.  <P>I had problems with the clutch switch on my first 85 SE slipping causing the switch to disconnect the cruise.  I ended up installing a small hose clamp on the back of the switch to keep it from sliding on it's mount and disconnecting me.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||jaguiler||04-17-2001||10:32 PM||||My stalk looks pretty old.  I will prob replace that first.  Thanks for all the suggestions!<P>John<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||04-17-2001||11:41 PM||||JUST got done pulling one off my donor and it was fairly easy<BR>just pull it straight out until it clicks rotate it around and pull it again it will just pop right out ||||reg||12
Z||000009||SLYGUY||09-20-2001||03:04 AM||||You didn't mention if your Fiero was a manul or automatic transmission. <P>If it is a stick, check this first...<P><BR>The clutch cancel switch may need adjusting. <BR>I couldn't even hear a click from the relay when I would turn the cruise on at the stalk. I read somewhere in here to check that switch on the clutch. <P>I turned the key on, flipped on the cruise....NOTHING.<P>Then I lifted the clutch pedal and repeated. <BR>I heard the cruise relay click.<P>All I had to do was adjust the switch by unplugging it at it's contact and turning it to give it a gap so that it thinks that the clutch is engaged and to enable the cruise.<P>Worked first time.<P>Can't tell you how nice that was to get working. Not that I can use it in California traffic much, but I just like to have everything working.<P>Got left headlight working in 20 minutes with a bushing rebuild kit from Rodney Dickman's fiero site. <BR>$8 and a little elbow grease and it saves me from having to raise the hood and lift the headlight by hand all the time.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
